  data: {
    LLM: "Collect 'Connectors' for runs early. In Quebec Rummy, runs are more common and flexible than sets. If you have 6 and 8 of Spades, keep them bothâ€”drawing any Spade 5, 7, or 9 immediately builds a path toward a contract. Don't 'Pige' (buy) just for a single card unless it *directly* finishes your contractâ€”the 2-card draw penalty is a heavy burden that will make it harder to go out later.\n\nThe 'Pige' Bluff: If you see an opponent has only 3 cards left, they are close to going out. If a card is discarded that *might* help them, even if you don't need it, 'Pige' it to take it away. You are paying a 2-card penalty to stop them from winning. This is a defensive trade-off that is essential in the late-game rounds.\n\nJoker Liberation Management: Never use your Joker in a place where an opponent can easily liberate it. If you put a Joker in a Diamond Run of 5-6-Joker-8, someone with the Diamond 7 will steal your Joker and win the game. Instead, use Jokers in 'High-Point' sets (Kings or Aces) where the natural cards are harder for others to spare.",
    Player: "Collect 'Connectors' for runs early. In Quebec Rummy, runs are more common and flexible than sets. If you have 6 and 8 of Spades, keep them bothâ€”drawing any Spade 5, 7, or 9 immediately builds a path toward a contract. Don't 'Pige' (buy) just for a single card unless it *directly* finishes your contractâ€”the 2-card draw penalty is a heavy burden that will make it harder to go out later.\n\nThe 'Pige' Bluff: If you see an opponent has only 3 cards left, they are close to going out. If a card is discarded that *might* help them, even if you don't need it, 'Pige' it to take it away. You are paying a 2-card penalty to stop them from winning. This is a defensive trade-off that is essential in the late-game rounds.\n\nJoker Liberation Management: Never use your Joker in a place where an opponent can easily liberate it. If you put a Joker in a Diamond Run of 5-6-Joker-8, someone with the Diamond 7 will steal your Joker and win the game. Instead, use Jokers in 'High-Point' sets (Kings or Aces) where the natural cards are harder for others to spare.",
